    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d368">

                <pb facs="0507" n="499" sameAs="frus:frus1941v04/pg499"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.94/2359</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of Conversation, by the Secretary of
                        State</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-10-03">October 3, 1941</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The British Ambassador called at his request. He said that his Government had
                    information to the effect that China, while voicing a contrary view, really
                    desired to see our talks with Japan prove successful. I replied that I had not
                    heard anything to this effect.<anchor n="93" xml:id="d368-f1"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>C[<hi rendition="#small-caps">ordell</hi>] H[<hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >ull</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d368-f1" n="93" xml:id="d368-f1-content">
                <p>In a second memorandum, the Secretary of State added that he gave the British
                    Ambassador “the high points” in the conversations with the Japanese in recent
                    months and requested that this information be not repeated by the British
                    Foreign Secretary (711.94/2360).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
